---
title: Data & remote functions
summary: All six of Kit's remote function primitives work inside islands, including query, command and form.
related:
  - regions/held-regions
  - app/spa-router
---

An island on its own cannot reach the server, yet most interactive parts of a page need to read data, change it or receive a stream of updates. Because islands are ordinary Svelte components (see [Regions](/docs/regions/regions)), Kit's remote functions work inside them without changes, and all six primitives (`query`, `query.batch`, `query.live`, `command`, `form` and `prerender`) run in an island exactly as they do in a Kit page. They take the place of the fetch calls and endpoints you would otherwise write by hand, and the server validates every call.

```svelte
<script>
  import { greeting } from './hello.remote';
  const who = await greeting();
</script>

<p>{who}</p>
```

In production, query results from the server render are seeded into the client cache, so an island that reads a query during SSR does not fetch it again when it hydrates. In dev this degrades to a refetch, as the note further down explains.

The seed is sized to its readers. A query result is seeded only when some island on the page can call that remote, which means its built chunks import the module. A lake, a server island or the page's own script that awaits a query only for the server render ships HTML and no seed, so a whole CMS entry rendered through a lake doesn't cost the page twice.

## Try it

This counter is a client island that reads a `query` and changes the value through two `command`s. The value lives on the server, so each button press is a real round trip, after which a `.refresh()` reads the value again. Because the state is shared, if you open this page in a second tab, both tabs move.

<script>
  import CounterDemo from './CounterDemo.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo">
  <CounterDemo />
</div>

```ts title=counter.remote.ts
let count = 3;
export const getCount = query(async () => count);
export const bump = command(async () => (count += 1));
```

## Forms and commands

`form()` and `command()` POST to their endpoints and work from inside an island. On success, `form()` runs Kit's `invalidateAll`, which ogygia treats as a **soft seed refresh**: it fetches the current page again, merges `<head>` and refreshes the data seeds in place, without a view transition, a body swap, an island remount or clearing live queries.

```svelte
<script>
  import { addEntry } from './guestbook.remote';
</script>

<form {...addEntry}>
  <input name="msg" />
  <button>Sign</button>
</form>
```

If an island needs a query to update after a mutation, call `.refresh()` on it, or pair `submit().updates(query)` with `requested(query).refreshAll()` on the server. `updates` on its own does not fill the response.

## Mutation guards

Writing to a captured host variable from an island's markup is a mistake, because the value is a serialized snapshot rather than live state. In dev, ogygia wraps each snapshot in a **deep proxy** that logs a `console.warn` with the prop's path whenever you mutate it. The write still applies, since this is only a warning, but treat it as the signal to move that state into the island.

::: warning Dev is not prod
In dev, SSR query seeding degrades to a refetch, and the mutation proxy only runs in dev. Check data flows against a production build.
:::

## Do / don't

- **Do** call `.refresh()` (or `submit().updates(q)` plus `requested(q).refreshAll()` on the server) when a mutation has to update a query. `updates` on its own does not fill the response.
- **Do** test data flows against a production build, because seeding and the mutation guard behave differently in dev.
- **Don't** mutate a captured host prop from an island's markup. It is a serialized snapshot, so move mutable state inside the island.

